        "original_id": "96c457f1-6a6f-5205-8666-fed6277b35b0",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Alberta, Canada, United Kingdom\n- Systems: Constitution of Alberta, Westminster system\n- Concepts: unwritten constitution, parliamentary procedure, laying before the house\n\nFacts:\n- The Constitution of Alberta describes the fundamental rules under which the Canadian province of Alberta is governed.\n- Alberta has an unwritten constitution, as is typical of Canadian provinces and Westminster systems.\n- The Westminster system is a parliamentary system of government modelled after the system that developed in the United Kingdom.\n- In parliamentary procedure, especially in the Westminster system, laying a document before the house means formally recognising that it has been made available for members of a deliberative assembly to read.",
